::ALG2_U02_L05_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L05_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which features are enough to start a polynomial sketch? {
~only the y-intercept #The y-intercept alone is not enough.
=zeros, multiplicities, and end behavior #Correct. These features guide the graph shape.
~only the constant term #The constant term alone is not enough.
~only one point #One point is not enough.
}

::ALG2_U02_L05_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L05_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>At x = -2 with multiplicity 2, what does the graph usually do? {
~crosses sharply #That is odd multiplicity behavior.
~has no intercept #It is still an x-intercept.
~becomes undefined #Polynomial graphs are defined there.
=touches and turns #Correct. Even multiplicity usually touches.
}

::ALG2_U02_L05_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L05_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>At x = 5 with multiplicity 1, what does the graph usually do? {
=crosses the x-axis #Correct. Multiplicity 1 is odd.
~touches only #That is even multiplicity behavior.
~has no x-intercept #It is an x-intercept.
~stops at the axis #Polynomial graphs continue.
}

::ALG2_U02_L05_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L05_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>For a positive cubic, what is the end behavior? {
~both ends rise #That is even positive behavior.
~both ends fall #That is even negative behavior.
=left falls, right rises #Correct. Odd degree positive leading coefficient falls left and rises right.
~left rises, right falls #That is odd negative behavior.
}

::ALG2_U02_L05_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L05_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What does a y-intercept help place on a polynomial sketch? {
=where the graph crosses the y-axis #Correct. The y-intercept is the point where x = 0.
~the degree only #Degree comes from the expression.
~all zeros #Zeros are x-intercepts.
~the leading coefficient #Leading coefficient comes from the expression.
}

